# Migration files are hashed by the migration runner: it records a sha256 when it
# applies a file and refuses to run if that file later differs, on the principle
# that a forward-only migration must never be edited.
#
# Git's autocrlf turns that safety check into a false alarm. On a Windows
# checkout every .sql arrives with CRLF, so the runner hashes different bytes
# from the ones the ledger recorded on Linux and reports a file as edited that
# nobody has touched. In fleet that blocked eleven pending migrations until it
# was diagnosed.
#
# The superproject declares `* text=auto`, but submodules do not inherit it.
# Pinning eol=lf makes the working copy byte-identical on every platform, so the
# checksum means what it claims to.
*.sql text eol=lf
